From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: The default DACL must name the token's own user

Patch 0002 gave every token the HKLM arrangement as its default DACL: Local
System and Administrators full control, Domain Users read. That is the right
shape for HKLM itself, and it is documented there as the fix for a bug where
naming the creating user made HKLM keys invisible to everyone else.

It is the wrong shape for a *token* default DACL, because it names everyone
except the one identity that matters. An object created by an ordinary user
gets a descriptor its own creator may read and not write. The user then
cannot create a subkey inside a key it just created, which is not a strict
permission model but a contradiction -- no ordering of operations satisfies
it.

Windows' default DACL names Local System and the user, for exactly this
reason. This adds the token's user back, keeping the property patch 0002
needed: users at large still only read, administrators still write, so HKLM
remains shared machine state.

The symptom was a long way from the cause. Wine's display setup creates
HKLM\System\CurrentControlSet\Control\Video\{guid}\0000 from the session
process. Refused at the last component, it generates a fresh guid and tries
again, forever: explorer at 100% of a core, a black screen, a desktop window
created but never named, and not one error message anywhere. A relay trace
is what named it -- RegCreateKeyExW returning 5 in a loop over changing
guids.

Verified in the image: explorer drops from 99.7% CPU to 0.1%, the desktop
window becomes "shell - Wine Desktop", and the Phase 0 gate passes 8/8 with
the machine-level wineserver running.

Forwarded: not-needed
--- a/server/token.c
+++ b/server/token.c
@@ -822,25 +822,39 @@
  * may write, everyone else may read. Per-user privacy is not this descriptor's
  * job -- HKCU gets that from being a separate hive per user.
  */
-static struct acl *sg_create_system_dacl(void)
+static struct acl *sg_create_system_dacl( const struct sid *user_sid )
 {
     struct sid alias_admins_sid = { SID_REVISION, 2, SECURITY_NT_AUTHORITY, { SECURITY_BUILTIN_DOMAIN_RID, DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_ADMINS }};
+    int own = user_sid && !equal_sid( user_sid, &local_system_sid );
+    unsigned int count = own ? 4 : 3;
     struct ace *ace;
     struct acl *dacl;
-    size_t size = sizeof(*dacl) + 3 * sizeof(*ace) +
+    size_t size = sizeof(*dacl) + count * sizeof(*ace) +
                   sid_len( &local_system_sid ) + sid_len( &alias_admins_sid ) +
-                  sid_len( &domain_users_sid );
+                  sid_len( &domain_users_sid ) + (own ? sid_len( user_sid ) : 0);
 
     if (!(dacl = mem_alloc( size ))) return NULL;
 
     dacl->revision = ACL_REVISION;
     dacl->pad1     = 0;
     dacl->size     = size;
-    dacl->count    = 3;
+    dacl->count    = count;
     dacl->pad2     = 0;
 
     ace = set_ace( ace_first( dacl ), &local_system_sid, ACCESS_ALLOWED_ACE_TYPE, 0, GENERIC_ALL );
     ace = set_ace( ace_next( ace ), &alias_admins_sid, ACCESS_ALLOWED_ACE_TYPE, 0, GENERIC_ALL );
+    /* The token's own user. Windows' default DACL names Local System and the
+     * user, and leaving the user out does not merely tighten things -- it
+     * produces an object its own creator may read and not write. A user then
+     * cannot create a subkey inside a key it just made, which is not a
+     * permission model but a contradiction.
+     *
+     * Wine's display setup is where this surfaced: it creates
+     * HKLM\System\CurrentControlSet\Control\Video\{guid}\0000 from the
+     * session process, is refused at the last component, and answers by
+     * generating a fresh guid and trying again -- forever, at 100% of a core,
+     * with a black screen and no error anywhere. */
+    if (own) ace = set_ace( ace_next( ace ), user_sid, ACCESS_ALLOWED_ACE_TYPE, 0, GENERIC_ALL );
     set_ace( ace_next( ace ), &domain_users_sid, ACCESS_ALLOWED_ACE_TYPE, 0, GENERIC_READ );
     return dacl;
 }
@@ -936,7 +950,7 @@
     struct sid alias_users_sid = { SID_REVISION, 2, SECURITY_NT_AUTHORITY, { SECURITY_BUILTIN_DOMAIN_RID, DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_USERS }};
     struct sid logon_sid = { SID_REVISION, 3, SECURITY_NT_AUTHORITY, { SECURITY_LOGON_IDS_RID, 0, 0 }};
     const struct sid *user_sid = security_unix_uid_to_sid( uid );
-    struct acl *default_dacl = sg_create_system_dacl();
+    struct acl *default_dacl = sg_create_system_dacl( user_sid );
